# ============================================================================
# validate_restore.rman
#
# Purpose:
#   Proves, without changing the database, that the backups on disk
#   are readable and complete enough to restore. Run this on a
#   schedule, not just after a backup failure, because a backup that
#   has never been validated is an assumption.
#
# Usage:
#   rman TARGET / @validate_restore.rman
#
# What each step checks:
#   CROSSCHECK BACKUP synchronizes the repository with what is
#   actually on disk, marking pieces that are gone as EXPIRED.
#   RESTORE DATABASE VALIDATE reads every backup piece a restore
#   would need and checks for physical corruption, without writing
#   any datafiles. CHECK LOGICAL adds a logical corruption check.
#   RESTORE ARCHIVELOG ALL VALIDATE does the same for the archived
#   logs a recovery would need.
#   REPORT NEED BACKUP lists datafiles that fall outside the
#   configured retention policy and need a fresh backup.
#
# What it does NOT do:
#   It does not restore anything. A clean VALIDATE means the pieces
#   are readable; it does not prove an end-to-end restore works.
#   That proof only comes from an actual test restore to an
#   isolated host, covered in docs/restore-validation-workflow.md.
#
# To validate one backup set by its key (from LIST BACKUP):
#   VALIDATE BACKUPSET <KEY>;
# Replace <KEY> with the key from the LIST output. That command is
# left out of the script body because it needs a real key.
#
# Notes:
#   Read-only with respect to the database. CROSSCHECK updates
#   repository status only. Reviewed; not executed against a live
#   Oracle instance.
# ============================================================================

CROSSCHECK BACKUP;

RUN {
  RESTORE DATABASE VALIDATE CHECK LOGICAL;
  RESTORE ARCHIVELOG ALL VALIDATE;
}

REPORT NEED BACKUP;
